Sprint 8 — Helm Quick Refresh
1. Check Helm
helm version

Check Helm repositories:
helm repo list

Add Bitnami if needed:
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

2. Create a Helm Chart
Create a working directory:
mkdir -p ~/helm-labs
cd ~/helm-labs

Create a chart:
helm create jfoods-nginx

Enter it:
cd jfoods-nginx

You will get:
jfoods-nginx/
├── Chart.yaml
├── values.yaml
├── charts/
└── templates/
    ├── deployment.yaml
    ├── service.yaml
    ├── serviceaccount.yaml
    ├── ingress.yaml
    ├── hpa.yaml
    ├── httproute.yaml
    ├── _helpers.tpl
    ├── NOTES.txt
    └── tests/

3. Important Chart Files
Chart.yaml
Contains chart metadata:
apiVersion: v2
name: jfoods-nginx
version: 0.1.0
appVersion: "1.16.0"

Remember:
Chart version → version of the Helm package
App version   → version of the application

4. values.yaml
This contains configurable/default values.
Our simple values:
replicaCount: 3

image:
  repository: nginx
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
  tag: "1.27"

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80

We later added:
config:
  APP_ENV: production
  LOG_LEVEL: info

And for the Secret exercise:
secret:
  username: demo-user
  password: demo-password

Do not use real passwords in values.yaml.
5. Helm Templates
Templates are under:
templates/

Example:
templates/deployment.yaml
templates/service.yaml
templates/configmap.yaml
templates/secret.yaml

Helm variables use:
.Values

Example:
replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}

If:
replicaCount: 3

Helm generates:
replicas: 3

6. Render the Chart — Very Important
Before installing:
helm template nginx-demo .

This does not change AKS.
It only renders:
values.yaml
     +
templates/
     ↓
Kubernetes YAML

Useful:
helm template nginx-demo .

or:
helm template nginx-demo . | less

Specific section:
helm template nginx-demo . | grep -A15 "kind: Deployment"

7. Create Namespace
We used a separate namespace for our Helm lab:
kubectl create namespace helm-demo

Check:
kubectl get namespace helm-demo

8. Install Helm Chart
From inside:
~/helm-labs/jfoods-nginx

run:
helm install nginx-demo . -n helm-demo

Here:
nginx-demo → Release name
.          → Current chart
helm-demo  → Kubernetes namespace

Check:
helm list -n helm-demo

9. Check Kubernetes Resources
kubectl get all -n helm-demo

Pods:
kubectl get pods -n helm-demo

Deployment:
kubectl get deployment -n helm-demo

Services:
kubectl get svc -n helm-demo

10. Helm Release
Remember:
Chart   = package / blueprint
Release = installed instance of the chart

Example:
Chart:
jfoods-nginx

Release:
nginx-demo

You can install the same chart multiple times:
helm install nginx-dev .
helm install nginx-prod .

Same chart, different releases.
11. Upgrade
Change something in values.yaml.
For example:
replicaCount: 5

Preview:
helm template nginx-demo .

Then upgrade:
helm upgrade nginx-demo . -n helm-demo

Check:
helm list -n helm-demo

You will see:
REVISION  2

Check pods:
kubectl get pods -n helm-demo

12. Helm History
helm history nginx-demo -n helm-demo

Example:
REVISION   STATUS
1          superseded
2          deployed

This is extremely important for production troubleshooting.
13. Rollback
Rollback to revision 1:
helm rollback nginx-demo 1 -n helm-demo

Then:
helm history nginx-demo -n helm-demo

Important:
Revision 1 → install
Revision 2 → upgrade
Revision 3 → rollback to 1

A rollback creates a new revision.
It does NOT change revision 3 back into revision 1.
14. --set
You can override values.yaml from the command line.
If:
replicaCount: 3

run:
helm template test-release . --set replicaCount=2

Helm renders:
replicas: 2

So:
values.yaml
     ↓
default = 3
     ↓
--set replicaCount=2
     ↓
override = 2

Useful commands:
helm install nginx-demo . --set replicaCount=5

or:
helm upgrade nginx-demo . --set replicaCount=5 -n helm-demo

15. ConfigMap Through Helm
We created:
values.yaml

with:
config:
  APP_ENV: production
  LOG_LEVEL: info

Then:
templates/configmap.yaml

contains the Kubernetes resource definition.
Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: jfoods-nginx-config
data:
  APP_ENV: {{ .Values.config.APP_ENV }}
  LOG_LEVEL: {{ .Values.config.LOG_LEVEL }}

Render it:
helm template nginx-demo .

Expected:
kind: ConfigMap
data:
  APP_ENV: production
  LOG_LEVEL: info

16. Use ConfigMap in Deployment
In deployment.yaml:
envFrom:
  - configMapRef:
      name: jfoods-nginx-config

Then after deployment:
kubectl exec -n helm-demo <pod-name> -- env | grep -E 'APP_ENV|LOG_LEVEL'

We got:
APP_ENV=production
LOG_LEVEL=info

So:
values.yaml
     ↓
configmap.yaml
     ↓
ConfigMap
     ↓
deployment.yaml
     ↓
Pod
     ↓
Environment variables

17. Secret Through Helm
This is the next part we were about to continue.
Basic structure:
values.yaml
     ↓
templates/secret.yaml
     ↓
Kubernetes Secret
     ↓
Deployment
     ↓
Pod

Use dummy values for learning:
secret:
  username: demo-user
  password: demo-password

And remember:
ConfigMap → non-sensitive configuration
Secret → sensitive configuration

Also:
Base64 encoding is not encryption.

18. Very Important Helm Commands
Keep this list handy:
helm version
helm repo list
helm repo update

helm create <chart-name>

helm show chart <chart>
helm show values <chart>

helm template <release> .

helm install <release> . -n <namespace>
helm list -n <namespace>

helm upgrade <release> . -n <namespace>

helm history <release> -n <namespace>

helm rollback <release> <revision> -n <namespace>

helm get values <release> -n <namespace>
helm get values <release> -n <namespace> --all

helm get manifest <release> -n <namespace>

helm uninstall <release> -n <namespace>

19. The Helm Mental Model
This is probably the most important thing to remember after your break:
                HELM CHART
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          │                   │
     values.yaml          templates/
          │                   │
          └─────────┬─────────┘
                    │
             helm template
                    │
                    ↓
           Kubernetes YAML
                    │
             helm install
                    │
                    ↓
             HELM RELEASE
                    │
          ┌─────────┴──────────┐
          │                    │
     Deployment              Service
          │
       ReplicaSet
          │
        Pods

And the lifecycle:
helm install
     ↓
Revision 1
     ↓
helm upgrade
     ↓
Revision 2
     ↓
helm rollback
     ↓
Revision 3
